# "This installation is alive, and here is what it thinks of itself."
#
#   sudo cp deploy/genmars-business-heartbeat.* /etc/systemd/system/
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
#   sudo systemctl enable --now genmars-business-heartbeat.timer
#
# Verify it is actually firing — an enabled timer that never fires is the
# usual way "we are monitoring it" turns out to be false:
#   systemctl list-timers genmars-business-heartbeat.timer
#   journalctl -u genmars-business-heartbeat.service -n 20
#
# ── IT NEEDS GENMARS_SYSTEM_KEY IN backend/.env ────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ ISSUE IT FIRST. INSTALLING THIS TIMER WITHOUT THE KEY IS A MAIL FLOOD.
#
#   No key → parent._post returns False → heartbeat() returns False →
#   report_health exits non-zero → OnFailure below fires genmars-alert@ →
#   notify.sh sends mail. Every five minutes. 288 emails a day, saying the
#   heartbeat failed.
#
#   The non-zero exit is deliberate and right — a heartbeat that fails
#   silently shows the dashboard "last seen hours ago", which reads as an
#   outage when only the reporting is broken. It just means the key has to
#   exist before the timer does. GM-INC-2026-0001 is what the flood costs:
#   alerts bounced, the provider suppressed the address, and thirty-one hours
#   of real alerts were dropped in silence.
#
# WHERE THE KEY COMES FROM — ops.genmars.co.ke/systems → the system → the
# "Reporting keys" panel → issue one. NOT Settings → Engineering, which is
# where sibling SIGN-ON is configured; this comment said that and it was
# wrong, and somebody went looking. The endpoint behind it is
# POST /api/ops/systems/<slug>/keys, guarded by CanManageAccess — founder
# only — and the token is shown EXACTLY ONCE. It is Argon2-hashed on the way
# in and cannot be read back; losing it means revoke and re-issue.
#
# ── AND THE ORDER MATTERS, BECAUSE `exec` IS NOT `run` ─────────────────────
# ExecStart uses `docker compose exec`, which runs inside the ALREADY-RUNNING
# container — and that container read `env_file` when it was created. Editing
# backend/.env is therefore not enough:
#
#   1. issue the key in ops
#   2. add GENMARS_SYSTEM_KEY= to backend/.env   (no sudo: it is mode 600 and
#      owned by the deploy user. `sudo -e` REFUSES it, because the directory
#      is user-writable — that is sudoedit's symlink guard, not a problem to
#      work around)
#   3. docker compose up -d api                  ← without this the container
#                                                  still has no key
#   4. docker compose exec -T api python manage.py report_health
#      ↳ it must print "reported — …". Only then install this timer.
#
# EnvironmentFile below is for THIS UNIT rather than for the container: the
# `docker compose` command itself needs POSTGRES_PASSWORD to interpolate
# compose.yaml, and without it every command in that directory fails before
# it reaches Docker.

[Unit]
Description=Business Platform — heartbeat to the Genmars system registry
Documentation=file:///opt/business-os/README.md
# Nothing to report if the application is not up.
Requires=docker.service
After=docker.service network-online.target

# The same handler the backups use, installed system-wide. %N names this unit
# so the mail says which job failed.
OnFailure=genmars-alert@%N.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/opt/business-os
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker compose exec -T api python manage.py report_health

# GENMARS_SYSTEM_KEY and APP_VERSION live here, beside everything else the
# container reads.
EnvironmentFile=/opt/business-os/.env

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
